# __TITLE__

Four sprites bouncing inside a window, written in TensionScript and drawn by the
in-core 2D renderer, packed as a Tension project.

```sh
npm install
npm run dev
```

A window opens. Change `SPEED` in `src/main.ts`, save, and the game restarts on
its own — the sprites move further per frame.

## What the commands do

- **`npm run dev`** builds the guest, packs the assets, runs it, and watches
  `src/`, `assets/` and `tension.config.json`. On a change it rebuilds and
  restarts. A build that fails leaves the running game alone, so a typo does not
  cost you the window.
- **`npm run build`** writes `dist/`: the wasm, the volume, the runtime binary,
  and a `run.sh` / `run.bat` launcher. That directory runs on its own — no Node,
  no compiler, no project.
- **`npm run doctor`** prints what resolves on this machine and what is missing.

Flags that matter: `npm run dev -- --headless` runs with no window (useful over
SSH), `--no-watch` runs once, `--build-only` builds and prints the command line
it would have run.

## The restart is a restart

`tension dev` kills the process and starts a new one; it does not reload the
guest inside the running one. **State is lost on every save.** There is no
snapshot format yet — when there is, this paragraph changes.

## The files

```
tension.config.json     the only project file that belongs to Tension
src/main.ts             the game
src/graph.generated.ts  the 2D pipeline blob (generated; do not edit)
assets/sprites.iwi      a four-cell sprite sheet, packed into build/assets.tns
tools/make-atlas.mjs    regenerates the sheet after you edit its palette
build/                  scratch: game.wasm, assets.tns, asconfig.json
dist/                   `npm run build` output
```

`tension.config.json` is read by the CLI and by nothing else. The runtime takes a
`.wasm` and no app configuration, so the CLI is what turns your window size, your
renderer and your assets into the runtime's arguments and environment. That is
also why the window size in the config reaches the guest as `--window=WxH` and is
read there by `windowRequest()`.

## Where the renderer lives

`renderer: "2d"` is built into the runtime. There is no adapter to install, no
library to find, and no environment variable to set — which is why this is the
default template.

If you want 3D, scaffold with `--template ogre` instead: that project loads the
OGRE adapter and needs the Ogre-Next libraries beside it, which `tension doctor`
reports on.

## The shape of the code

`src/main.ts` has five steps and they are always in this order:

1. **Open the session.** Nothing that touches guest memory works before it;
   without it the canvas opens and the first verb answers `-EBADF`.
2. **Ask for a window.** The window is the guest's to create, and
   `windowedOrOffscreen` falls back to an offscreen target when there is no
   display.
3. **Set the pipeline.** The blob is compiled into the guest and must be one the
   adapter was built with; `tension dev` emits it from the runtime, so it always
   is.
4. **Load the atlas** out of the volume. `assets/` is mounted at `/`, so
   `assets/sprites.iwi` is `/sprites.iwi`.
5. **Present frames** until `present` answers `-ECANCELED`, which is what a
   closed window looks like.

Records are `struct` declarations that fill themselves — `records[i].set(...)` —
and the buffer is sized with `sizeof<SpriteRecord>()`. There are no byte offsets
in this file, and adding one would be a second, unchecked copy of a layout the
compiler already knows.

## If the window does not open

Run `npm run doctor`. The two failures worth knowing:

- **No display.** `doctor` says so, and `npm run dev -- --headless` runs the
  game offscreen for a bounded number of frames.
- **A missing library.** The runtime links the system's SDL3; on a distribution
  that ships only SDL2, `doctor` names the library the loader could not find.
